{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-14100",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "Since we have an overall common factor of $2$, we start by dividing our equation through by $2.$ This gives \n\n$$\n10{y}^{3}+5{y}^{2}-16y-8=0.\n$$\n\nNext, we factor by grouping. Notice that when grouping the smaller terms, we factor out the negative sign.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10{y}^{3}+5{y}^{2}-16y-8 & = \\\\ (10{y}^{3}+5{y}^{2})-(16y+8) & = \\\\ 5{y}^{2}(2y+1)-8(2y+1) & = \\\\ (2y+1)\\cdot 5{y}^{2}-(2y+1)\\cdot 8 & = \\\\ (2y+1)(5{y}^{2}-8) & .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we set each factor equal to $0$ and solve:\n\n- Setting the factor $(2y+1)$ equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2y+1 & =0 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Setting the factor $(5{y}^{2}-8)$ equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{y}^{2}-8 & =0 \\\\ 5{y}^{2} & =8 \\\\ y & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{8}{5}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,  the solutions to the given equation are $-\\frac{1}{2}$ and $\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{8}{5}}.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
